## backend architecture

The backend does not implement perfectly but strives towards modular (domain-oriented) monolith architecture.
It is decomposed into the following modules:
- user (registration, login, retrieval by username and id, updating, etc.)
- task (uploading, listing, updating tasks (statement, tests, execution limits, metadata, etc.), also importing and exporting to .ZIP)
- exec (arbitrary code execution and result streaming; actuall execution is don by the "tester")
- lang (as of now just has hardcoded list of available programming languages, their compilation, execution commands, etc.)
- subm (user solution to problem submission. the most important module. stores subm., uses exec to evaluate and stream back results)

Each module is currently implemented a little bit differently but generally flows through the following 3 layers:
1. http (gateway layer - marshalling JSON requests, responses, authorization (admin,user,guest), caching GET requests, mapping error codes)
2. srvc (abbreviation of service. use-case orchestration layer - logging, error reporting, persistance orchestration, calling external services)
3. repo (persistance layer - atomic, consistent, isolated, durable storage in postgres database)

Some modules may also incorporate a "domain" layer outside of `srvc`. On the file system, it could be located at the root of a module implementation.
The "domain" layer can often be skipped by defining aggregates, domain services, etc. in the service layer.

Services are used through a "facade" interface. The main purpose - gather methods that service exposes as a way of documentation.
Moreover, if the monolith is to refactored into seperately deployable microservices, facade can handle remote interaction.

For observability, currently we strive towards implementing contextual logging by appending attributes such as "http-req-uuid", "module", "layer".

Unit tests are kept alongside code in `_test.go` files but integration tests may be extracted to `tests` directory at the root of the module.
Unit tests have mocked or minimal dependencies whereas integration tests may call external systems. Testing the persistence layer is an integration test, imo.

Interestingly, I've decided that the HTTP layer of each module may aggregate data from multiple services to present frontend with necessary information.

Each module has a srvc.go file somewhere that defines through an interface the service contract for it's bounded context.

